  "query_preview": "..(Monday through Saturday)\nIssued at 510 PM EDT SAT AUG 15 2026\n\nThe long term period will open with upper level ridging over the \nsouthern CONUS and troughing over the Northern Plains into the \nUpper Great Lakes region, maintaining northwest flow aloft. \nMeanwhile, at the surface, an area of low pressure will be located\nin the vicinity of western Kentucky, with a frontal boundary \nsitting just west of the area. This boundary will push through the\nforecast area as a cold front on Monday, bringing renewed shower \nand storm chances. As in previous days, the environment will be \nvery moist, with PW values generally in the range of 1.7-1.9 in. \nThermodynamic profiles also look to be supportive of torrential \ndownpours, with a deep warm cloud layer and LREF mean MUCAPE in \nthe range of 2000-2500 J/kg across the forecast area. Given these \nindicators and the expectation that soils will be fairly \nsaturated, isolated flash flooding will be possible. In fact, WPC \nhas placed the entire forecast area in a Day 3 Marginal (Level \n1/4) Risk for Excessive Rainfall to cover this flash flood threat.\nRain chances will peak Monday afternoon into evening; then, \nfollowing the frontal passage, drier air will advect in from the \nnorth. By Tuesday, dewpoints will be only in the mid to upper 60s,\nwith perhaps a few areas in the 70s in the southwestern corner of\nthe forecast area. Highs will also be marginally cooler, \ngenerally in the mid to upper 80s, and rain chances are looking to\nbe low. Of note, a few recent model runs do depict subtle \nshortwave energy traversing the area, which could inspire a few \nshowers or storms, although this scenario still appears unlikely \nat this time.\n\nThe next robust rain chances will arrive Wednesday evening into \nthe overnight hours. Aloft, troughing will continue to dig into \nthe Great Lakes region, and the ridge to the south will continue \nto flatten. Meanwhile, an additional cold front will push \nsoutheast, with rain chances ticking up again late Wednesday. \nMoisture will return quite quickly as winds become southwesterly \nahead of the front, and PW values look to leap again too (with \nLREF mean PW in the 1.6-1.7 in range for much of the area, \nrepresenting at least the 75th percentile compared to \nclimatology). As in previous days, this environment could support \nat least an isolated flash flooding risk; WPC has again placed the\nmajority of the forecast area in a Day 5 Marginal (Level 1/4) \nRisk for Excessive Rainfall (covering Wednesday morning through \nThursday morning). \n\nBehind the front, temperatures will be noticeably cooler. With\neastern Kentucky sitting near the base of the upper level trough,\nflow aloft will either be zonal or northwesterly, with models \nstill disagreeing about the exact orientation of the trough at \nthis stage in the forecast. Regardless, this pattern will open \neastern Kentucky up to additional shortwave impulses. Although \nexact timing is difficult to resolve at this timescale, broad rain\nchances have been maintained each day through the end of the \nperiod for now.",
